---
// About — §4.4. First-person voice. The tagline, shop description, and
// owner bio below are Saida's real words from her Etsy About section. The
// four deeper story sections (what "meros" means, growing up between NJ
// and Uzbek tradition, why she started, what she wants the pieces to do)
// are an initial draft written in that same voice — each marked with a
// DRAFT comment so Saida can tell at a glance what's hers verbatim versus
// what she may want to personalize further.
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/content/Hero.astro';
import StoryBlock from '../components/content/StoryBlock.astro';
import ImageBleed from '../components/system/Image.astro';
import saidaPortrait from '../assets/about/saida-portrait.jpg';

const products = await getCollection('products');
const scarfProduct = products.find((product) => product.id === 'atlas-ikat-neck-scarf') ?? products[0];
const heroImage = scarfProduct.data.images.find((image) => image.type === 'detail') ?? scarfProduct.data.images[0];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Meros Silkworks"
  description="The story behind Meros Silkworks, in Saida's own words."
>
  <Hero image={heroImage.src} alt={heroImage.alt}>
    <h1>About Meros</h1>
    <p>Rooted in Tradition. Designed for now.</p>
  </Hero>

  <div class="page">
    <div class="about">
      <figure class="about__photo">
        <ImageBleed src={saidaPortrait} alt="Saida, the maker behind Meros Silkworks" width={900} height={1125} />
      </figure>

      <div class="about__copy">
        <p class="about__byline">Sagida (Saida / Sai)</p>

        <p class="about__intro">
          Hi! My name is Sagida, though most know me as Saida or Sai. I am absolutely elated to be
          able to share my culture in modern spaces and bring you all along with me.
        </p>

        <StoryBlock title="Uzbek roots, worn every day">
          <p>
            Accessories honoring the diaspora that hope to connect culture with their everyday look.
            Each piece is handmade and cared for with love, attention, and generations of
            craftsmanship — Uzbek roots with a global spirit.
          </p>
        </StoryBlock>

        <section class="about__section">
          <h2>What "meros" means</h2>
          {/* DRAFT — Saida to personalize further if she wants */}
          <p>
            Meros is the Uzbek word for inheritance — not just what gets handed down, but what you
            choose to carry forward and make your own. I named the shop after it because that's
            exactly what this fabric is to me: something inherited, handwoven by people who came
            before me, that I get to carry into rooms and lives it was never made for. Every piece
            that leaves this shop is a small piece of that inheritance, reshaped for wherever you're
            taking it.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="about__section">
          <h2>Between New Jersey and Uzbek tradition</h2>
          {/* DRAFT — Saida to personalize further if she wants */}
          <p>
            I grew up between two worlds that rarely spoke to each other — Uzbek at home, New Jersey
            everywhere else. For a long time that felt like something to explain rather than
            something to wear. Meros Silkworks is what happens when I stopped translating my culture
            for other people and started just showing it to them instead: the same atlas ikat my
            family has always worn to weddings and celebrations, cut into something you'd reach for
            on an ordinary Tuesday.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="about__section">
          <h2>Why I started</h2>
          {/* DRAFT — Saida to personalize further if she wants */}
          <p>
            It started small — one bow, made from a scrap of atlas silk I couldn't bring myself to
            throw away, worn just to see what people would ask about. They asked a lot. That was the
            moment I realized this fabric didn't need to stay folded away for special occasions only
            — it could live in daily life, in someone else's closet, carrying a piece of Uzbekistan
            with it. Meros Silkworks grew out of that one bow.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="about__section">
          <h2>What I want these pieces to do</h2>
          {/* DRAFT — Saida to personalize further if she wants */}
          <p>
            More than anything, I want you to feel like you're wearing something that means something
            — not a costume, not a trend, but a real piece of a real place and a real history that
            now belongs a little bit to you too. If it starts a conversation, if it makes you feel a
            little more rooted or a little more seen, if it becomes the thing someone asks about when
            you walk into a room — that's the whole point.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: var(--page-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    /* No bottom padding: Footer supplies its own space-24 top margin, so
       adding one here would double the gap above it. */
    padding: var(--space-16) var(--page-pad) 0;
  }

  .about {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about__photo {
    max-width: 24rem;
  }

  .about__byline {
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caps);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-ink);
  }

  .about__intro {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .about__copy > :global(.story-block) {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about__section {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .about__section h2 {
    font-size: var(--text-lg);
  }

  .about__section p {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: 24rem 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-12);
    }
  }
</style>
